<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Loire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Loire is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 1,000 km long. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> and flows north and then west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> to the Atlantic at Saint-Nazaire. In antiquity many Gallic peoples lived along it, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a frontier and a highway. Its valley, lined with royal châteaux, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/loire.jpg" alt="Loire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cramos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Loire_%C3%A0_La_Bohalle_par_Cramos.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Loire is 1,006 kilometres long and drains 117,054 square kilometres, more than a fifth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It rises near Mont Gerbier de Jonc in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cevennes">Cévennes</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardeche">Ardèche</a>, flows north through Nevers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, then west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> at Saint-Nazaire. Its largest tributary is the Allier, at 410 km, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, the Cher and the Indre. Its name comes from the Latin Liger, itself from a Gaulish word meaning silt or sediment. It gives its name to six departments, and it flows with few dams or locks, floods usually coming in February and March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Studies of the region suggest that the early Loire flowed north and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, until the lower Loire, flowing west, captured it and produced the present river. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> used it as a trading route by 600 BCE, linking the metals of the Armorican Massif with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Cenabum</a>, which they named Aurelianis, and began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Caesarodunum</a>, now Tours. Christianity spread through the basin from the 3rd century. In 408 the Alans, under King Sangiban, crossed the river and settled along its middle course.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> the Loire marked the border between the French and the English. Joan of Arc's relief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> in 1429 was the turning point of the war. Under François I, who reigned from 1515 to 1547, the Italian Renaissance deeply influenced the valley's architecture. River traffic was long hindered by floods and droughts; in the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> had stone retaining walls and quays built from Roanne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>. Floods in 1707 were said to have drowned 50,000 people. In 1700 Nantes had more inland waterway craft than any other French port, but railways killed off the surviving traffic from the 1850s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The middle stretch, about 280 km long, is known as the Garden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and holds over a thousand châteaux, among them Amboise, Chambord, Chenonceau and Villandry, many begun as feudal strongholds. Count Foulques Nerra of Anjou built defensive keeps there between 987 and 1040. The stretch between the Pays de la Loire and Centre-Val de Loire regions was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage list on 2 December 2000. Vineyards run from Muscadet near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> to Sancerre, and Anjou, Saumur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a> and Vouvray lie between.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Loire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Great, who ruled from the 990s until 1030, made the dukes among the most respected princes of the kingdom, and his son Guy-Geoffrey, who reigned as William VIII, acquired the Duchy of Gascony in the 1050s and 1060s, extending ducal lordship from the <b>Loire</b> to the Pyrenees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The great majority were peasants growing grain, with wine in the <b>Loire</b> valley, Bordeaux and Burgundy, under seigneurs who collected dues and held rights of justice long after serfdom had largely disappeared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 486 Clovis defeated Syagrius, the Roman ruler of the Domain of Soissons, and took over northern Gaul as far as the <b>Loire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 460s a Saxon leader named Adovacrius (Odovacer) fought on the lower <b>Loire</b> around Angers until the Frankish king Childeric I took the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of wars with the Suebi, took Emerita Augusta in 469, captured Hispania Tarraconensis, the last bastion of Western Roman rule in Spain, in 472, and by 476 ruled to the Rhone and the <b>Loire</b>, holding Arles and Marseille; Gallo-Roman and Hispano-Roman aristocrats served him as generals and governors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the Tagus estuary the first moves were by sea: south into the Mediterranean, with enclaves in south-western Spain, southern France and the Po Valley, and north to Brittany, from where routes along the <b>Loire</b> and Seine brought maritime beakers to the Lower Rhine about 2600 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
